<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heinrich Heine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1797">1797</a>–<a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Harry Heine, Christian Johann Heinrich Heine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heinrich Heine was a German poet, writer and critic, born Harry Heine into a Jewish family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>, who converted to Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 1825. His early lyric poetry, collected in the Buch der Lieder of 1827, was set to music by Schubert, Mendelssohn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-schumann">Robert Schumann</a> and others; his later verse and prose are marked by satire and irony. Associated with the Young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> movement, he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1831, and in 1835 the German Diet banned his works with theirs; bedridden from 1848, he died in Paris in 1856. Nazi Germany burned and later banned his books, and a line from his play Almansor on burning books and people is engraved at the site of the 1933 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> book burning.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-heine.jpg" alt="Heinrich Heine" width="330" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moritz Daniel Oppenheim · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_Heine-Oppenheim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Heine was born on 13 December 1797 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>, then in the Duchy of Berg, the eldest of four children of the textile merchant Samson Heine and Peira, née van <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geldern">Geldern</a>. He was called Harry in childhood. His uncle Salomon realised that he had no talent for trade, and it was decided that he should study law, so in 1819 he went to the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>. There he was a radical liberal, but he was more interested in history and literature, heard the critic August Wilhelm Schlegel lecture, and began to acquire a reputation as a poet. He also wrote two tragedies, Almansor and William Ratcliff, which had little success in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>It was extremely difficult to make a living as a writer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and Heine never had enough money. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> in January 1826 he met Julius Campe, who remained his chief publisher for the rest of his life. Books under 320 pages had to be submitted to censorship, so Campe printed dissident works in large type to run longer. Heine's travel writings, the Reisebilder, were a success. He left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1831, drawn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a>, by freedom from German censorship and for a time by Saint-Simonianism. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> he became a celebrity, counting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin">Frederic Chopin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a> among his acquaintances, and wrote everything in German, translating it into French with a collaborator.</p><p>After the German Diet banned the Young Germany writers in November 1835, his name was added at Metternich's insistence, but he went on commenting on German politics from a distance. In the 1840s his verse became more political and satirical. The most popular of his political poems was The Silesian Weavers, based on the weavers' uprising of 1844.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In May 1848 Heine fell paralysed and did not leave what he called his mattress-grave until his death on 17 February 1856. A hair analysis in 1997 confirmed chronic lead poisoning. Many composers set his poems, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-schumann">Robert Schumann</a> in the song cycle Dichterliebe and Friedrich Silcher in his setting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorelei">Die Lorelei</a>. The Nazis removed or destroyed all monuments to him and, from 1940, banned his books. His line from Almansor, spoken after the burning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, was engraved at the site of the 1933 book burning on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>'s Opernplatz.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heinrich Heine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…recorded four theories: that Luther and his companions sang it entering Worms for the Diet in April 1521 (<b>Heinrich Heine</b>); that it honoured Luther's friend Leonhard Kaiser, executed on 16 August 1527 (K. F. T. Schneider); that the Lutheran princes sang it entering Augsburg for the Diet of…</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-mighty-fortress-is-our-god">A Mighty Fortress Is Our God</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1824 <b>Heinrich Heine</b> took up Brentano's theme in &quot;Die Lorelei&quot;, one of his most famous poems.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lorelei">Die Lorelei</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.23,6.78&amp;year=1835" aria-label="Düsseldorf on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Heinrich Heine"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5362 5729h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5362 5729h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.23,6.78&amp;year=1835">What was here in 1835? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The thought goes before the deed, as the lightning precedes the thunder.&#8221;</p><footer>Religion and Philosophy in Germany (1834) &#183; tr. S. L. Fleishman, revised, 1887 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37478/pg37478-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A drama will be enacted in Germany in comparison with which the French Revolution will appear a harmless idyl.&#8221;</p><footer>Religion and Philosophy in Germany (1834) &#183; tr. S. L. Fleishman, revised, 1887 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37478/pg37478-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…freedom is a new religion, the religion of our age. If Christ is not the God of this religion, he is still one of its high-priests, and his name shines consolingly in the hearts of its children.&#8221;</p><footer>English Fragments (1828), The Liberation &#183; tr. Charles Godfrey Leland, revised, 1887 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37478/pg37478-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Paris is the New Jerusalem, and the Rhine is the Jordan which separates the land of Freedom from the land of the Philistines.&#8221;</p><footer>English Fragments (1828), The Liberation &#183; tr. Charles Godfrey Leland, revised, 1887 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37478/pg37478-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
